I investigated issue #4423, which explains that API responses are incorrectly using the first acceptable language from an `Accept-Language` header, ignoring the quality values. This is evident in requests like `Accept-Language: en;q=0.3, fr-FR;q=0.9`, which should prefer French but return English responses.

The responsible code is in `src/i18n/index.ts`, in the `pickLocale` function. Currently, this function does not account for quality (`q`) values in the `Accept-Language` header:

```typescript
/** Best supported locale from an Accept-Language header, e.g. "fr-CA,fr;q=0.9,en;q=0.8". */
export function pickLocale(header: string | undefined): Locale {
  if (!header) return DEFAULT_LOCALE;
  for (const part of header.split(",")) {
    const language = part.split(";")[0].trim().slice(0, 2).toLowerCase();
    if (isLocale(language)) return language;
  }
  return DEFAULT_LOCALE;
}
```

This code simply chooses the first locale it recognizes from the header, bypassing any specification of preferences through `q` values.

Thus, the issue is definitely a bug in the `pickLocale` function logic of `src/i18n/index.ts`.

